1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:03,469
Paris, 1988.

2
00:00:04,019 --> 00:00:09,180
Thousands of passengers arrived at Charles
de Gaulle Airport, collected their

3
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:11,727
luggage, and continued their journeys.

4
00:00:12,277 --> 00:00:13,629
But one man stayed.

5
00:00:14,179 --> 00:00:16,577
His name was Mehran Karimi Nasseri.

6
00:00:17,127 --> 00:00:20,947
And for the next eighteen years, this
airport would become his home.

7
00:00:21,497 --> 00:00:24,486
His story would one day inspire a
Hollywood film.

8
00:00:25,036 --> 00:00:28,344
The real one is quieter, sadder, and far
less certain.

9
00:00:31,544 --> 00:00:39,031
Nasseri was born in Iran, most likely in
1945, in the oil town of Masjed Soleyman.

10
00:00:39,581 --> 00:00:44,453
Much of what we know about his early life
comes from his own telling, and his

11
00:00:44,453 --> 00:00:46,414
telling changed over the years.

12
00:00:46,964 --> 00:00:50,676
He said he had studied in England in the
1970s.

13
00:00:51,226 --> 00:00:57,635
He said he had been expelled from Iran in
1977, after protesting against the Shah.

14
00:00:58,185 --> 00:01:03,507
At different times, he said his mother was
Scottish, or Swedish. Neither claim was

15
00:01:03,507 --> 00:01:04,480
ever confirmed.

16
00:01:05,030 --> 00:01:09,279
Journalists who later looked into his past
questioned parts of that account.

17
00:01:09,829 --> 00:01:16,239
What is better documented is this: in
1981, he was recognized as a refugee in

18
00:01:16,239 --> 00:01:16,905
Belgium.

19
00:01:17,455 --> 00:01:20,558
For several years, he lived there, and
travelled in Europe.

20
00:01:23,758 --> 00:01:26,981
Then, in 1988, he set out for Britain.

21
00:01:27,531 --> 00:01:30,651
What happened next depends on whose
account you read.

22
00:01:31,201 --> 00:01:36,646
In the version most often reported, his
passport and his refugee certificate were

23
00:01:36,646 --> 00:01:38,058
stolen along the way.

24
00:01:38,608 --> 00:01:41,942
In other versions, he sent the papers away
himself.

25
00:01:42,492 --> 00:01:45,287
Either way, he arrived in England without
them.

26
00:01:45,837 --> 00:01:50,851
British officials refused him entry and
returned him to the airport he had flown

27
00:01:50,851 --> 00:01:52,355
from: Charles de Gaulle.

28
00:01:52,905 --> 00:01:56,443
In France, he now had a problem with no
simple solution.

29
00:01:56,993 --> 00:01:58,772
He could not prove who he was.

30
00:01:59,322 --> 00:02:05,271
And so, in August 1988, a man without
papers sat down in Terminal 1.

31
00:02:08,471 --> 00:02:12,012
It is often said that he was trapped in a
sealed transit zone.

32
00:02:12,562 --> 00:02:14,034
That is not quite right.

33
00:02:14,584 --> 00:02:20,047
Reports place him in a public area of the
terminal, where travellers, cleaners and

34
00:02:20,047 --> 00:02:22,245
police walked past him every day.

35
00:02:22,795 --> 00:02:24,181
He was not locked in.

36
00:02:24,731 --> 00:02:26,806
What held him in place was paperwork.

37
00:02:27,356 --> 00:02:28,722
Weeks became months.

38
00:02:29,272 --> 00:02:30,722
Months became years.

39
00:02:31,272 --> 00:02:35,412
His case moved slowly between lawyers,
officials and governments.

40
00:02:35,962 --> 00:02:39,818
And as it did, a bench in Terminal 1
became an address.

41
00:02:43,018 --> 00:02:44,651
His routine was precise.

42
00:02:45,201 --> 00:02:48,448
He washed and shaved each day in the
public bathrooms.

43
00:02:48,998 --> 00:02:54,719
He kept his belongings close: a luggage
trolley, boxes and bags, stacked carefully

44
00:02:54,719 --> 00:02:56,045
beside a red bench.

45
00:02:56,595 --> 00:02:58,819
He read newspapers and magazines.

46
00:02:59,369 --> 00:03:00,930
He studied economics.

47
00:03:01,480 --> 00:03:05,169
And he wrote, every day, in a diary he
kept for years.

48
00:03:05,719 --> 00:03:07,912
Airport workers came to know him well.

49
00:03:08,462 --> 00:03:11,690
Some brought him food. Some brought him
newspapers.

50
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:15,143
The airport doctor gave him paper to write
on.

51
00:03:15,693 --> 00:03:20,703
To them, he became a familiar figure, and
he took on a new name: Sir Alfred.

52
00:03:23,903 --> 00:03:27,978
By the 1990s, the man on the bench had
become a story.

53
00:03:28,528 --> 00:03:32,532
Journalists from around the world came to
Terminal 1 to interview him.

54
00:03:33,082 --> 00:03:37,679
Photographers made his portrait among the
passengers and departure boards.

55
00:03:38,229 --> 00:03:45,730
His life inspired a French film, Lost in
Transit, in 1993, and an English opera,

56
00:03:45,730 --> 00:03:47,230
Flight, in 1998.

57
00:03:47,780 --> 00:03:50,598
He answered questions politely, and
patiently.

58
00:03:51,148 --> 00:03:56,750
In 2004, a journalist writing for The
Guardian spent time with him, and found

59
00:03:56,750 --> 00:04:01,042
that key parts of the story he told could
not be confirmed.

60
00:04:01,592 --> 00:04:06,216
But the more he was asked about his past,
the less settled that past became.

61
00:04:09,416 --> 00:04:12,842
In 2004, the story reached Hollywood.

62
00:04:13,392 --> 00:04:18,495
Steven Spielberg's film The Terminal
starred Tom Hanks as Viktor Navorski, a

63
00:04:18,495 --> 00:04:21,248
traveller stranded at a New York airport.

64
00:04:21,798 --> 00:04:23,837
The film was not a biography.

65
00:04:24,387 --> 00:04:28,202
It moved the story to America and invented
its own hero.

66
00:04:28,752 --> 00:04:35,916
But DreamWorks had acquired the rights to
Nasseri's story, for a sum widely reported

67
00:04:35,916 --> 00:04:37,536
at around $250,000.

68
00:04:38,086 --> 00:04:43,066
The same year, his memoir, The Terminal
Man, was published under the name Sir

69
00:04:43,066 --> 00:04:43,972
Alfred Mehran.

70
00:04:44,522 --> 00:04:50,106
A man who owned almost nothing now had a
film deal, a book, and a famous address.

71
00:04:53,306 --> 00:04:56,566
The strangest part of the story is what
happened next.

72
00:04:57,116 --> 00:05:01,484
In the mid-1990s, Belgium indicated he
could return there.

73
00:05:02,034 --> 00:05:05,987
Later, France offered him papers that
would have allowed him to stay.

74
00:05:06,537 --> 00:05:08,128
He refused to sign them.

75
00:05:08,678 --> 00:05:13,901
According to reports, the documents
identified him as Iranian, under his

76
00:05:13,901 --> 00:05:14,916
original name.

77
00:05:15,466 --> 00:05:20,143
He wanted to be recognized differently: as
Sir Alfred, and as British.

78
00:05:20,693 --> 00:05:25,330
So the man who had waited years for papers
turned down the ones he was offered.

79
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:30,766
What began as a legal problem had become,
at least in part, a choice.

80
00:05:33,966 --> 00:05:40,817
In July 2006, after nearly eighteen years,
he fell ill and was taken to hospital.

81
00:05:41,367 --> 00:05:43,724
His corner of Terminal 1 was cleared.

82
00:05:44,274 --> 00:05:47,779
When he was discharged, he did not go back
to the airport.

83
00:05:48,329 --> 00:05:51,580
Reports say he later lived in a shelter in
Paris.

84
00:05:52,130 --> 00:05:57,004
Accounts of the years that followed are
sparse, and they do not always agree.

85
00:05:57,554 --> 00:06:03,303
For the first time since 1988, Sir Alfred
was no longer living in the terminal.

86
00:06:06,503 --> 00:06:08,702
Sixteen years later, he came back.

87
00:06:09,252 --> 00:06:14,865
In September 2022, according to the
airport, he returned to live in a public

88
00:06:14,865 --> 00:06:16,785
area of Charles de Gaulle.

89
00:06:17,335 --> 00:06:23,104
On 12 November, around midday, he suffered
a heart attack in Terminal 2F.

90
00:06:23,654 --> 00:06:27,288
He died there, of natural causes,
according to the airport.

91
00:06:27,838 --> 00:06:29,726
He was in his late seventies.

92
00:06:32,926 --> 00:06:34,696
Airports are built for movement.

93
00:06:35,246 --> 00:06:39,191
Millions of people pass through them, and
almost no one stays.

94
00:06:39,741 --> 00:06:41,871
Mehran Karimi Nasseri stayed.

95
00:06:42,421 --> 00:06:46,938
Part of his story was a failure of
paperwork, and part of it may always

96
00:06:46,938 --> 00:06:47,892
remain his own.

97
00:06:48,442 --> 00:06:53,526
He came to Charles de Gaulle as a man
without documents, and he is remembered as

98
00:06:53,526 --> 00:06:55,814
the traveller who never really left.
